  "query_preview": "..\nIn the upper levels, a ridge is centered over Texas and the \nsouthern plains today while Tropical Storm Bertha begins to\ncross the Texas Gulf Coast and Hurricane Fausto approaches\nHawaii in the Pacific. Though mean southeasterly flow continues\ninto southern Arizona via the ridge to the east, the placement \nof this ridge has warmed temperatures aloft, suppressing \nthunderstorm chances today to just isolated coverage (10-15 \npercent) across southeastern Arizona. Satellite derived \nprecipitable water values this afternoon range from around 1 \ninch in the far southeast to over 1.5 inches in southwest \nArizona, adequate for at least a few thunderstorms to form with \nbrief heavy rainfall and a 30-40 mph wind gust threat. As the \nridge center nudges west on Friday so will the thunderstorm \nchances with just slight chances (10-20 percent) expanding west\ninto central- western Pima. \n\nThe discussed westward shift of the upper ridge will also bring\na heating trend to southeastern Arizona. High temperatures will\nbecome 5-10 degrees above normal Friday and Saturday, ranging\nfrom 105-112 in the Tucson Metro, Western Deserts, and Upper \nGila River Valley. In lower elevation areas of Santa Cruz and\nCochise counties highs range from 100-105. Can't rule out highs\ncoming in a bit hotter than currently forecast with the strength\nof the ridge moving overhead. NBM probabilities give a 33%\nchance for the Tucson airport to hit 110 degrees on Saturday.\nAdditionally notable will be the overnight lows with forecast \nlows from Tucson west ranging from 82 to 88 degrees Saturday\nmorning. The lack of relief from the heat plus the afternoon\nhighs combine to produce Major HeatRisk with the Extreme Heat\nWarning in effect Friday and Saturday. \n\nThe ridge center is expected to lift north after shifting west\nand become centered closer to the Four Corners region on\nSaturday, putting southeastern Arizona under a more favorable \npattern for thunderstorm activity. After a period of modest\ndrying through Friday, moisture return should arrive in earnest\nthrough Saturday, partially driven by potential northerly driven\noutflows from northern Mexico thunderstorm activity and the\ncrossing remnants of Tropical Storm Bertha. The combination of\nthis incoming moisture with Saturday's hot temperatures and some\nmodest mid-level flow will provide an environment for strong to\nsevere thunderstorms capable of severe wind gusts, blowing dust,\nand heavy rainfall rates. In collaboration with SPC, a Day 3\nMarginal Risk for severe thunderstorms has been introduced on\nSaturday for much of southeastern Arizona. \n\nThe upper ridge will remain in a similar spot for the coming\ndays into next week, bringing daily chances for thunderstorms\nacross the region with day to day variability. This will also\nbring temperatures down closer to normal by Monday. Looking down\nthe line, ensembles bring the upper ridge center into Arizona\nand amplify the ridge by the end of next week, which would\nincrease temperatures and reduce thunderstorm chances, depending\non where this ridge center sets up.",
